# evidence-for: US-006, US-007, FR-012, FR-019, FR-020, FR-021, FR-023, FR-039
#
# The ATTRIBUTION and EQUIVALENCE half of the sweep: every clause of those two
# shapes in scripts/survey.py, scripts/llmstxt.py, tests/test_survey.py and
# tests/test_llmstxt.py, measured against the code or against git before it was
# left standing. Six failed and are corrected in this commit on top of the two
# the defects cite; the rest held. The COVERAGE half is in
# evidence/casting-4-red-first-measured.log.
#
# Corrected (numbers refer to the sections below):
#   1. test_survey.py said `[1, 2]` kills the survey because "every read of it
#      below is pkg.get". Section 1 lists the reads: two of them are not --
#      `if pkg:` and `pkg[key]`. What the claim needs is the FIRST read below
#      the load, which is pkg.get, and that is what it now says. survey.py's
#      own load_json docstring was already right about this.
#   2. test_survey.py called run_script "the one fixture-shaped helper this
#      suite is allowed (GI-004)" in two places, and test_llmstxt.py called it
#      "the one helper this suite shares". Section 2 shows conftest.py hands
#      out three fixtures; fixture_repo and build_repo are fixture-shaped too,
#      and both modules use fixture_repo. GI-004 constrains the helper that
#      RUNS A SCRIPT, and all three sentences now say that.
#   3. test_survey.py asserted that user_surface.commands is "the only list
#      that says" a flag is one the product declares. Section 3: doctype.py
#      draws SURVEY_ALLOW from labels, screens and commands, and checks
#      LENS_ALLOW beside it. The assertion now says what the test measures --
#      commands is the array survey.py publishes a declared flag into.
#   4. test_survey.py said the flag pass uses "the join the decorator pass
#      already uses, cap and all" -- D-136's false equivalence restated inside
#      the test. Both copies now name the difference; the measurement is in
#      evidence/casting-4-join-completion-test.log.
#   5. test_survey.py's note on test_a_flag_named_only_in_prose_is_not_a_command
#      ended "and from this cycle on it is a guard", using a word the same
#      docstring defines as green at every revision measured. That row is red
#      at cfafe8e, a802345, 4ae7334, 1ea2cab and 19941ad, measured in the
#      red-first log. The note now says it is green from d7eb572 on and says
#      why the column keeps 19941ad rather than "guard".
#
# Left standing, measured:
#   4/5. llmstxt.py's strip_comments docstring says three routes reach a reader
#      and all three come through it; section 4 shows the four call sites those
#      three routes are (frontmatter title, frontmatter description, body, and
#      one_line). The header chain's "five sources feed the header and all five
#      are authored" is the five one_line calls on the same listing; the sixth,
#      os.path.basename(ROOT), is the one that is not cleaned.
#   5. readme_summary says it reads for the marker "the same way the page loop
#      reads for it": the same expression, twice, at llmstxt.py:192 and :234.
#      The marker does sit inside an HTML comment, as scaffold.py:138 writes it.
#   6. survey.py's "the two branches have different floors and both are counted
#      here the same way" -- {12,110} after a capital is thirteen, {3,110}
#      after a capital is four, as the comment states. The pyproject cli
#      entries are in the same {name, target, anchor} shape as the package.json
#      bin entries above them.
#   7. survey.py's module docstring says the narrowed census "was corrected in
#      both places at once, by deleting that copy". 81235d1 is one commit and
#      it changed both.
#   8. It also says the comment copy was narrowed "a cycle before it was
#      narrowed here". e4e0775 carries the narrowed comment with the flat
#      docstring; d383851 carries both narrowed. e4e0775 is a cycle-8 fix and
#      d383851 a cycle-9 one, by the strip-evidence commits that bound them.
#   9. Both docstrings count the tests that do not run the script -- two in
#      test_survey.py, one in test_llmstxt.py, each saying those are the only
#      ones.
#  10-11. Every "(added SHA)" and "(added cycle N)" in the two tables, against
#      the first commit whose diff introduces that test. Reading cycle numbers
#      off the strip-evidence commits that bound them: 1ea2cab falls in cycle
#      3, d7eb572 in 4, 737d31a in 5, 3aa48f7 in 6, 20b15d1 in 7, e4e0775 in 8,
#      d383851 in 9, and anchor_census's 81235d1 in 10 -- which is what the two
#      "added cycle N" columns say. The rows naming a commit rather than a
#      cycle (4ae7334, b520ca9) are checked against that commit.
#
